\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Physical histories, from the dense reference. (a) Variance: monotone decay without forcing, a plateau with it. (b) The budget of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:budget} formed numerically; the unforced flows close it to within the line width, and the forced case approaches closure as the steady state is reached, evaluated at the stored snapshots since the production $\langle \theta f\rangle $ is not a recorded diagnostic. (c) Centroid $\bar  k$ and the $95\%$ wavenumber $k_{95}$ move to small scale and stall, which is the cascade reaching $\ell _B$. (d) Gradient flatness rises well above the Gaussian value $3$ as filaments form. (e) The variance spectrum filling in. (f) Scalar dissipation, peaking as the cascade arrives at $\ell _B$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:histphys}{{1}{3}{Physical histories, from the dense reference. (a) Variance: monotone decay without forcing, a plateau with it. (b) The budget of Eq.~\eqref {eq:budget} formed numerically; the unforced flows close it to within the line width, and the forced case approaches closure as the steady state is reached, evaluated at the stored snapshots since the production $\langle \theta f\rangle $ is not a recorded diagnostic. (c) Centroid $\bar k$ and the $95\%$ wavenumber $k_{95}$ move to small scale and stall, which is the cascade reaching $\ell _B$. (d) Gradient flatness rises well above the Gaussian value $3$ as filaments form. (e) The variance spectrum filling in. (f) Scalar dissipation, peaking as the cascade arrives at $\ell _B$}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {III}Time histories}{3}{section*.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Compression histories at $L=10$, $N=10$, bond cap swept. (a) The error rises while scales are being generated and then saturates rather than drifting. (b) The same curves against the error of merely representing the exact field at that budget, a bounded factor below. (c) Rank: the intrinsic rank of the exact solution at three probe accuracies (solid) plateaus far below the geometric bound, while the rank carried by the solver (dotted) sits at its cap throughout, $\epsilon $ being off. (d) Stored parameters, carried against needed. (e) The variance error, two to four orders of magnitude below the pointwise error at the same budget. (f) Mass drift: truncation is not mass-conserving and no projection is applied, so $|\langle \theta \rangle |$ grows by an amount set by the budget, against $10^{-15}$ for the dense reference.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:histcomp}{{2}{4}{Compression histories at $L=10$, $N=10$, bond cap swept. (a) The error rises while scales are being generated and then saturates rather than drifting. (b) The same curves against the error of merely representing the exact field at that budget, a bounded factor below. (c) Rank: the intrinsic rank of the exact solution at three probe accuracies (solid) plateaus far below the geometric bound, while the rank carried by the solver (dotted) sits at its cap throughout, $\epsilon $ being off. (d) Stored parameters, carried against needed. (e) The variance error, two to four orders of magnitude below the pointwise error at the same budget. (f) Mass drift: truncation is not mass-conserving and no projection is applied, so $|\langle \theta \rangle |$ grows by an amount set by the budget, against $10^{-15}$ for the dense reference}{figure.2}{}}
